(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacodynamics-exam-2-antimicrobials-quiz-3"]={"title":"Antimicrobials Quiz — Set 3","category":"Pharmacodynamics","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which of the following is an anti-pseudomonal penicillin?","choices":["Penicillin G","Amoxicillin","Nafcillin","Piperacillin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Piperacillin (with ticarcillin) is an anti-pseudomonal penicillin. — Slide 30"},{"q":"Which describes the correct killing pattern for beta-lactams?","choices":["Concentration-dependent with prolonged PAE","Concentration-dependent with no PAE","Bacteriostatic with moderate PAE","Time-dependent with no PAE"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beta-lactams are bactericidal with time-dependent killing and no PAE. — Slide 35-36"},{"q":"Which agent covers gram-positive anaerobes, gram-negative anaerobes, and parasites?","choices":["Vancomycin","Ciprofloxacin","Cephalexin","Metronidazole"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Metronidazole covers gram-positive and gram-negative anaerobes plus parasites. — Slide 64"},{"q":"A patient develops flushing during a rapid IV infusion of an antibiotic. Which drug is most likely responsible?","choices":["Amoxicillin","Doxycycline","Vancomycin","Levofloxacin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vancomycin causes red man syndrome/flushing when infused rapidly. — Slide 45"},{"q":"Beta-lactams and vancomycin share which general target?","choices":["The bacterial ribosome","The bacterial cell wall","Bacterial DNA gyrase","Bacterial folate synthesis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both target the cell wall, though by different mechanisms (PBP inactivation vs. blocking cross-linking). — Slide 33, 43"},{"q":"Which macrolide has additional activity against Haemophilus influenzae and is more effective against Legionella and H. pylori?","choices":["Clarithromycin","Erythromycin","Clindamycin","Penicillin V"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Clarithromycin adds activity against H. influenzae and is more effective against Legionella and H. pylori. — Slide 54"},{"q":"Which agent is bacteriostatic?","choices":["Metronidazole","Vancomycin","Ciprofloxacin","Doxycycline"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tetracyclines like doxycycline are bacteriostatic. — Slide 49"},{"q":"As one moves UP in cephalosporin generation, gram-positive activity generally:","choices":["Increases","Disappears entirely at 2nd generation","Stays exactly the same","Decreases (until 4th gen restores it)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Gram-positive activity decreases with advancing generation, while 4th generation restores it without sacrificing gram-negative coverage. — Slide 39"},{"q":"Which is a specific indication for oral vancomycin?","choices":["Treatment of Rocky Mountain spotted fever","Severe antibiotic-associated C. difficile colitis","Treatment of bacterial vaginosis infections","Treatment of community-acquired pneumonia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Oral vancomycin is limited to severe antibiotic-associated C. difficile colitis. — Slide 44"},{"q":"Aztreonam (a monobactam) is bactericidal and its mechanism is to:","choices":["Inhibit DNA gyrase","Bind the 50S ribosomal subunit","Inactivate penicillin-binding proteins","Block cell wall cross-linking as a glycopeptide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. As a beta-lactam, aztreonam inactivates PBPs to disrupt cell wall synthesis. — Slide 29, 33"},{"q":"Which agent is used for acne, atypical pneumonia, and animal-borne diseases such as RMSF and Lyme?","choices":["Aztreonam","Ceftriaxone","Doxycycline","Vancomycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Doxycycline is used for acne, atypical pneumonia, and animal-borne diseases including RMSF and Lyme. — Slide 50"},{"q":"Which class is bactericidal, time-dependent, with moderate PAE, and covers gram-positives only?","choices":["Glycopeptides (vancomycin)","Fluoroquinolone agents","Tetracycline class agents","Monobactam antibiotics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vancomycin is bactericidal, time-dependent, with moderate PAE, gram-positive only. — Slide 44"},{"q":"Which fluoroquinolone should NOT be used for skin infections per the lecture?","choices":["Levofloxacin","Ciprofloxacin","Moxifloxacin","Gemifloxacin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ciprofloxacin is specifically noted as NOT for skin. — Slide 60"},{"q":"The natural penicillin spectrum includes which gram-negative cocci?","choices":["Neisseria species","Escherichia coli","Klebsiella species","Pseudomonas aeruginosa"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Penicillin V covers gram-negative cocci such as Neisseria gonorrhoeae and N. meningitidis. — Slide 35"},{"q":"Which agent is bactericidal and covers gram-positives, gram-positive bacilli (e.g., Corynebacterium diphtheriae), and spirochetes?","choices":["Penicillin V","Ciprofloxacin","Doxycycline","Metronidazole"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Penicillin V is bactericidal and covers gram-positive cocci/bacilli and spirochetes. — Slide 35"},{"q":"Which distinctive adverse effect is linked to fluoroquinolones and warrants caution in children?","choices":["Cartilage/tendon damage","Tooth discoloration","Red man syndrome","Disulfiram-like reaction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fluoroquinolones are associated with cartilage/tendon damage, prompting caution in children. — Slide 62"},{"q":"Which class inhibits translocation steps of protein synthesis by binding the 50S subunit?","choices":["Aminoglycoside agents","Macrolides and clindamycin","Tetracycline class agents","Beta-lactam antibiotics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Macrolides and clindamycin inhibit translocation by binding the 50S subunit. — Slide 52"},{"q":"Which combination product increases anaerobe coverage (Bacteroides) and covers MSSA?","choices":["Aztreonam given as monotherapy alone","Penicillin V given as monotherapy alone","Cefdinir given alone as monotherapy","Amoxicillin + clavulanic acid (Augmentin)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Augmentin (amoxicillin + clavulanic acid) increases anaerobe coverage and covers MSSA. — Slide 42"},{"q":"Which agent is preferred for CAP among the fluoroquinolones and adds Pseudomonas coverage?","choices":["Levofloxacin","Ciprofloxacin","Nalidixic acid","Ofloxacin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Levofloxacin is used for CAP with added Pseudomonas coverage and improved gram-positive activity. — Slide 61"},{"q":"Which is TRUE about macrolides' bactericidal/static classification?","choices":["Always strictly bacteriostatic, regardless of dosage used","Always fully bactericidal, regardless of the dose given","Bacteriostatic, but may be bactericidal at higher doses","Neither; it purely inhibits bacterial toxin release only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Macrolides are bacteriostatic but may be bactericidal at higher doses. — Slide 52"},{"q":"Cefazolin (Ancef) and cephalexin (Keflex) are examples of which generation?","choices":["1st generation","2nd generation","3rd generation","4th generation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cefazolin and cephalexin are 1st-generation cephalosporins. — Slide 31"},{"q":"Which agent is indicated for vaginal infections such as bacterial vaginosis and trichomoniasis?","choices":["Doxycycline","Metronidazole","Cephalexin","Vancomycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Metronidazole treats BV and trichomoniasis. — Slide 64"},{"q":"Clindamycin's spectrum includes gram-positive aerobes, gram-positive anaerobes, and:","choices":["Atypical pathogens","Gram-negative aerobes","Pseudomonas species","Gram-negative anaerobes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Clindamycin covers gram-negative anaerobes (but not gram-negative aerobes). — Slide 55"},{"q":"Which agent is used for surgical prophylaxis in penicillin-allergic patients and for endocarditis and osteomyelitis?","choices":["Ciprofloxacin","Metronidazole","Vancomycin","Amoxicillin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vancomycin is used for surgical prophylaxis (penicillin allergy), endocarditis, and osteomyelitis. — Slide 44"},{"q":"Which class is bactericidal and inhibits DNA replication and transcription?","choices":["Macrolide agents","Tetracyclines","Fluoroquinolones","Glycopeptides"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fluoroquinolones are bactericidal and inhibit DNA replication/transcription via DNA gyrase and topoisomerase. — Slide 59"},{"q":"Which agent would you AVOID giving with dairy, antacids, or iron because of chelation?","choices":["Tetracyclines (doxycycline)","Vancomycin (given orally)","Metronidazole tablets","Aztreonam given by injection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tetracyclines chelate cations, so dairy, antacids, and iron should be avoided. — Slide 51"},{"q":"Which agent is preferred (among macrolides) as it is less active against streptococci/staphylococci but far more active against respiratory H. influenzae and M. catarrhalis?","choices":["Erythromycin","Azithromycin","Clindamycin","Penicillin G"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Azithromycin is less active against strep/staph but far more active against respiratory H. influenzae and M. catarrhalis. — Slide 54"},{"q":"Which beta-lactam class is most resistant to certain beta-lactamases compared with penicillins?","choices":["Anti-staphylococcal penicillins","Natural penicillins","Cephalosporins","Extended-spectrum penicillins"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cephalosporins are more resistant to certain beta-lactamases than penicillins. — Slide 38"},{"q":"Which agent is bactericidal and has anti-protozoal/anti-parasitic effects in addition to antibacterial activity?","choices":["Doxycycline","Metronidazole","Cephalexin","Erythromycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Metronidazole is bactericidal and has anti-protozoal/anti-parasitic effects. — Slide 63"},{"q":"Ceftriaxone (Rocephin) is which generation of cephalosporin?","choices":["1st generation","3rd generation","2nd generation","4th generation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ceftriaxone is a 3rd-generation cephalosporin. — Slide 31"}]};
